 "answer": "Air quality in Kuala Lumpur is sitting in the **\"Unhealthy\"** range (with readings around 110 to 135+ across areas like Cheras and Batu Muda). \n\nWhether your kids should skip school or weekend classes depends on their health and the latest government guidelines:\n\n### 1. What the Official Rules Say (MOE Guidelines)\nThe Ministry of Education (MOE) reverted to the National Haze Action Plan (PTJK):\n* **School Closures (API > 200):** Schools in KL are **not** mandated to close unless the official Air Pollutant Index (API) hits 200. \n* **No Outdoor Activities (API > 100):** Because KL’s API is over 100, all outdoor classes, sports, and co-curricular activities must be suspended.\n* **Parental Discretion Allowed:** Education Minister Fadhlina Sidek confirmed that **parents are officially permitted to keep their children at home** if they are worried about the haze, provided you notify the school administration beforehand. *(Note: Since it is Saturday, this also applies if they have weekend tuition, replacement classes, or co-curricular events).*\n\n### 2. Should You Keep Them Home?\n**Keep them at home if:**\n* **They have respiratory issues:** If your children have asthma, allergies, bronchitis, or any underlying heart/lung conditions, an API above 100 poses a high risk of triggering symptoms.\n* **The school is poorly ventilated:** Many local classrooms rely on open windows and fans rather than air conditioning with HEPA filtration, meaning indoor school air will be just as hazy as the air outside.\n* **The event is outdoors:** If they are only going in for Saturday sports or uniform body activities, they should stay home (and the school should have already canceled or moved these indoors).\n\n**It is likely okay to send them if:**\n* They are completely healthy and experiencing no coughing, throat irritation, or watery eyes.\n* Their classrooms are enclosed, air-conditioned, and equipped with air purifiers. \n* You can send them with a well-fitted protective mask (like a child-sized KN95 or KF94) for their commute, and ensure they bring plenty of water to stay hydrated.\n\n**Next Steps:**\nIf you want to double-check the exact reading for your specific neighborhood before making a call, check the official Department of Environment tracker at **[apims.doe.gov.my](http://apims.doe.gov.my)**. If you decide to keep them home, just send a quick message or letter to their teacher to let them know it is due to the haze.",
